<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balingen is a town on the river Eyach in the Zollernalb district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a>. It was founded as a town by the counts of Zollern in the mid-thirteenth century and belonged to their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schalksburg">Schalksburg</a> line until it was sold, with the Schalksburg lordship, to Württemberg in 1403.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balingen.jpg" alt="Balingen" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Lothar42 at German Wikipedia. Uploaded to Commons by User Sanbec on Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balingen_Zollernschloss.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It had already been narrowed by an earlier division, for a twelfth-century branch of the family had become the counts of Hohenberg, and it would be divided again when a line based on the Schalksburg near <b>Balingen</b> split off around 1288.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
